WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:03.878
Bittersweet moment, watching Kyle
win a championship, uh, watching

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:07.173
the city that you love winning a
championship, but yet at the same

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.675
time, flipping on your phone and seeing

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.555
people say, “Well, DeMar left and
Kawhi came in and that's why they won.”

00:00:13.888 --> 00:00:14.639
Take me through that.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:37.120
- Man, HBO Max, partner
and a sponsor in "Chips."

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:40.498
That's love. Check this out.

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:43.710
♪♪♪
- We are House Atreides.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.753
There is no call

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:48.006
we do not answer.
- What's to become of our world?

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:53.428
♪♪♪
- Are you part of this family or not?
- The revolution will be televised.
♪♪♪

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.224
- What's up, everybody?

00:00:58.224 --> 00:00:59.684
Welcome back to “Chips.”

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:03.563
You know, when doing a show like this,
I think the most fun part for me is

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.483
when I am able to have friends of mine on.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.736
So I'm welcoming the special guest today.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.447
My brother, DeMar DeRozan.
What's up, brother?

00:01:12.447 --> 00:01:13.031
- What's up, dawg?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.199
Appreciate you for having me.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.704
- There were many stories linking you
to LeBron, linking you to the Lakers.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:23.500
Then I saw something link
you to the Clippers. And ultimately,

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:24.959
you know,

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:28.463
I had said in my mind, like, there's
a high possibility, you know,

00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:30.924
you, you may want to go back home.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:38.473
Uh, most importantly, just to be close to
Moms obviously, uh, with Pops passing

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:41.810
away, which, again, sorry for the loss.

00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:42.811
I know that's tough.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:43.728
I can't imagine.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.650
Um, ultimately you
decided to go to Chicago.

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:50.652
Why did you end up saying, all right.

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:51.194
You know what?

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:52.403
I'm not doing that.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.866
Um, was it those two teams didn't
have the right amount of money?

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:58.993
Was it you just saw an
opportunity in Chicago?

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:02.288
What was—
- Yeah, I mean, it
was, it was a real possibility.

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:06.543
Um, I mean, I'd be lying to you if
I didn't say I wanted to come on,

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:09.462
you know? I did try to make it happen.

00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:12.132
There's a real possibility
on it happening.

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:15.301
Um, you know, just things didn't
work out, you know what I mean?

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:18.972
But, you know, um, Chicago was always

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:22.433
in my top five. That
was always on my radar.

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:23.309
You know what I mean?

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:31.317
They make the move to get Lonzo. Great
pickup. Been a fan of his. And, and you

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:37.031
see the narrative change with Zach wanting
to stay if I came and obviously Vuc there

00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:40.160
and, you know, we just build off that
to where it's like, all right, this

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.786
thing would really be a possibility.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:46.416
Then on the back end of it, it's
a hell of a city, hell of a city.

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:47.125
- Absolutely.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.753
Hell of an organization to where,
like, you get this thing winning here,

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:54.966
It's, it's—come on.

00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:58.761
- Let's talk about the journey. Uh,
start, you know, growing up in Compton.

00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:00.763
Just take me back to those days.

00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:03.892
And how did Compton
shape who you are today?

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:07.687
- Um, I mean, obviously growing up in the
environment you grew up in, no matter how

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.691
bad or how good a situation
may be growing up in it,

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:12.525
you think it's normal?
- Mmhmm.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:16.696
- You know, you try to be a kid
as long as you can, but it kind

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:17.947
of forced you to grow up quick.

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:21.326
The city built me, made me resilient;
at the same time, gave me the

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:25.330
imagination to want to do something
bigger than just be in a neighborhood.

00:03:25.788 --> 00:03:27.874
- You're still back there all the time.

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:31.419
You're still showing love
to, to where you come from.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.673
I mean, you're, you're essentially
still just one of the guys.
- For me,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.845
I remember being a kid and I remember
seeing like, uh, a terrible NBA player,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.806
like, the, the 15th guy on the roster.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.229
I remember seeing him at a young age in
L.A., and it felt like I just seen Jesus Christ.
- Mmhmm.

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:50.730
- It gave me hope.

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:52.232
And it gave a lot of people around me

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:52.523
hope.

00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:55.693
So for me, I always just try
to go back and just be normal.

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:56.319
And a lot of time not even

00:03:57.153 --> 00:03:58.279
be on some preaching stuff.

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:02.450
Just to be around just to be around.
- You had opportunities to go play in

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.746
other places, further outside of L.A.,
probably to Oak Hill or anywhere else.

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:11.751
Why was it so important to you to stay
home and go to your public high school?

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:16.839
- Um, I'd be lying, lying to you if I told
you, like, I wasn't scared to leave home.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.383
Like, I was scared as shit.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.177
Like, I was terrified,
you know what I mean?
- Which is funny.

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:25.473
- Like, this, this was all I knew, you
know what I mean? Family, friends, everything.

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:28.393
I couldn't imagine going to Oak Hill.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:29.143
You know what I mean?

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:30.728
I was terrified, you know what I mean?

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:35.650
Then secondly, it was like, man, this is
where I’m from. My high school is terrible.

00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:37.568
You know, it's down the
street from my house.

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:38.361
I can walk there.

00:04:38.695 --> 00:04:43.741
It's a terrible school to go to,
but it was just the challenge in me

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.120
saying like, man, let me go here and
just try to make something out of it.

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:47.745
- It's funny

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:48.162
as hell

00:04:48.204 --> 00:04:52.583
to hear you sit here and say I
was terrified to leave Compton.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.627
Most people are terrified
to go to Compton.
- Right. Yeah.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:05:01.175
- Then you can go to any college in
America and you decide to go to USC.

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:05.179
You know, USC is USC, but even at the time
you was leaving to go there,

00:05:05.179 --> 00:05:08.641
it wasn't viewed as a basketball
powerhouse, and you could've went anywhere.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:12.270
Why USC?
- Same reason. Same reason, bro.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:16.274
I was terrified to leave home. And the
crazy thing with that, even when I went

00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:18.693
to USC, I had such a culture shock.

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:21.154
My first three months in college,
I still was living in Compton.
- Really?

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:23.865
- I still, I still was
back and forth, you know?
- And how far is that?

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:25.950
- Twenty-five minutes.
- OK.

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:26.909
- You know what I mean?

00:05:27.035 --> 00:05:30.330
I still was back and forth because I
was just—just the culture shock was

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:35.126
just completely something I couldn't,
like, adjust to, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:39.130
- So that just goes back to me just
being terrified to leave home.

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:39.797
You know what I mean?

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:47.472
- So as a, as a kid, essentially, 18,
19 years old, heading into the draft,

00:05:48.264 --> 00:05:49.140
it's possible

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.351
you could end up in Michigan.

00:05:52.226 --> 00:05:55.772
You could end up in Florida, Louisiana.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:01.402
Not only do you end up far away from
L.A. You end up in another country.

00:06:01.486 --> 00:06:02.779
- Complete other country, bro.

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:03.780
- Take me there.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.949
Take me draft night,
getting drafted to Toronto.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:09.118
What's the first thing that
go to your, go through your mind?

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:10.745
- I didn't have a passport.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:11.245
I didn't have none of that shit.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.291
Threw me off. Like, damn, nobody

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:16.626
I know has a passport.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:21.005
- You go through draft night, you get to
draft night, you get drafted by Toronto

00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:26.219
and you still don't have a passport?
- I got my passport two days before the draft

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:26.719
- Wow.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:28.471
- to go to Toronto.

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:32.058
I remember going up there by myself
that next morning. Go to Toronto.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:35.603
I'm seeing shit in an airport in French.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:39.023
I'm like, I'm like losing it.

00:06:39.023 --> 00:06:40.191
You know, you know how it is.

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:40.358
- Yeah.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.320
- There go, like, the whole
culture shock all over again.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:45.071
You know what I mean?

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:48.241
And this time around it's somewhere
where I can't just go home.

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:49.117
You know what I mean?

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.743
I didn't have nobody with me.

00:06:50.827 --> 00:06:51.786
I was by myself.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.081
I remember I was still
wearing Chucks in the wintertime.

00:06:55.498 --> 00:06:57.875
I didn't know how to dress.
- Never been to the cold.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:58.918
- I’d never been to the cold.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:00.169
I’d never been to the cold.
- Mm.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:02.296
- Nobody there to really teach me.

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:03.923
I'm the only rookie on the team.

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:08.970
So it ain't like I got somebody I can hang
with at all times and blah, blah, blah.

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:12.640
So it was a, it was a shock,
you know what I mean?

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.811
And luckily at one point, Jarrett
Jack took me in and really helped

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:18.729
me get through my rookie year.

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:23.484
Him, Chris Bosh, Reggie Evans,
those guys really took me in and

00:07:23.568 --> 00:07:26.404
kind of helped me because, bra,
I was, I was lost for a minute.

00:07:26.487 --> 00:07:26.946
You know what I mean?

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:27.363
I was lost.

00:07:27.864 --> 00:07:31.117
- What's so interesting about that
is most people don't understand.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:36.164
They hear you saying, like, I got drafted to Toronto,
but at that time, 13 years

00:07:36.164 --> 00:07:37.748
ago, Toronto is not what it is today.

00:07:38.499 --> 00:07:42.420
So you walking through the airport.
There’s French signs on the highway,

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.630
It's like, it's very Americanized now.

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:46.632
- Yeah. Yes.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.718
- But at that time—
- It was tough, bro.

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:49.385
I ain't gonna lie.

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:52.722
It was, it was, it was the
toughest time that was necessary

00:07:52.722 --> 00:07:54.682
for me because I hadn't really

00:07:55.725 --> 00:07:58.644
been snatched away to be
like, huh, figure it out.
- Mmhmm.

00:07:58.769 --> 00:07:59.270
- You know what I mean?

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.106
I always had that crutch to
go back home and be back home.

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:05.109
So my whole entire year I was by myself.

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:07.069
I didn't have family with me, nothing.

00:08:07.111 --> 00:08:09.280
Like, I was by myself, figuring it out.

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:12.617
- You signed a new deal:
four years, $38 million.

00:08:13.951 --> 00:08:17.538
And at that time, by the way,
at that time, four years, 38

00:08:17.538 --> 00:08:19.790
is a lot of money in the NBA.
- That was a lot. A lot. A lot.

00:08:20.625 --> 00:08:23.336
- During that same time you
signed the same amount Steph,

00:08:24.295 --> 00:08:25.713
uh, Ty Lawson.

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:28.466
- Who else was there?
- Jrue Holiday.

00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:34.180
Um, a lot of y'all at the same
time sign, like, the same deal.

00:08:34.972 --> 00:08:38.017
And that's when that was a
lot of money in the NBA.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:39.560
That's life-changing money.

00:08:39.602 --> 00:08:39.894
- Yeah.

00:08:40.436 --> 00:08:42.939
- How did you adapt to that?

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:51.739
- For me, it was more so like, just not
like—I wanted to be respected as a player.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:56.953
Not by the amount of money that I'm
getting, you know what I mean?

00:08:57.078 --> 00:09:02.833
And I think when that happened,
I kinda had a different

00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:04.043
type of chip on my shoulder.

00:09:04.126 --> 00:09:08.172
It pushed me to a different type of
level to where like, damn the money. Now

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:09.840
I just got to step up to another level.
- Mmhmm.

00:09:10.091 --> 00:09:10.591
- You know what I mean?

00:09:10.591 --> 00:09:11.425
Like, I got that.

00:09:11.676 --> 00:09:12.843
I'm not even worried about that.

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.428
I'm not even speaking on that.

00:09:14.428 --> 00:09:18.266
I just wanted to be the player
and be respected by my peers on

00:09:18.266 --> 00:09:21.644
the court.
- We fast forward a little bit. Time in Toronto,

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:28.484
leading scorer, breaking many records,
All-Star, all that, which is by the way,

00:09:28.484 --> 00:09:33.281
people don't understand, you know, there's
the documentary on Vince Carter and how

00:09:33.656 --> 00:09:36.158
that helped grow Toronto basketball.

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:41.664
But then you come right after that
and building everything up, being

00:09:41.664 --> 00:09:45.710
a cornerstone of the franchise.
You meet your best friend,

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:51.549
Kyle Lowry. Y'all did great things in Toronto
expecting to keep building and keep going up.

00:09:52.967 --> 00:09:57.430
And then you're traded eventually.

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:03.269
How did you feel about that
process and where did that take

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:05.104
you with the game of basketball?

00:10:05.313 --> 00:10:11.360
- Um, with the whole process, it was like,
you know, you—my whole mindset was to

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.824
build Toronto up to be a respected team
because I played my first couple years,

00:10:17.783 --> 00:10:20.661
motherfuckers used to come in there,
especially on the weekend games,

00:10:20.661 --> 00:10:26.167
it'd be like, you know, should we go on
out night before? We playin’ the Raptors

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:29.378
at one o'clock on a Sunday. That’s a sure win.

00:10:29.378 --> 00:10:31.714
We out all night. We winnin’.

00:10:31.714 --> 00:10:32.173
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:10:32.173 --> 00:10:34.675
- Like, teams used to come
in with that mentality.

00:10:34.717 --> 00:10:38.262
So for me, it was always personal.

00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:40.473
Like, I wanted us to be respected,

00:10:40.514 --> 00:10:41.932
the organization be respected,

00:10:41.932 --> 00:10:45.686
the city be respected. When the situation
happened to me, just getting traded,

00:10:47.188 --> 00:10:47.647
it hurt.

00:10:47.688 --> 00:10:52.401
It hurt like shit because, you
know, my whole mindset was like, yo,

00:10:52.568 --> 00:10:54.070
I'm riding, I'm riding and dying.

00:10:54.236 --> 00:10:58.074
Like, like, only way I don't
want accomplish what I'm

00:10:58.074 --> 00:10:58.908
trying to accomplish here is

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:02.203
if, if, if, if it's on a
stretcher. That was my mentality.

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:05.665
So when it happened, it hit me hard.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:06.415
You know what I mean?

00:11:06.415 --> 00:11:10.461
Like, a lot of people always think
like, you know, I was bitter, I was this and

00:11:10.461 --> 00:11:14.757
all this, like, nah, it fucked me up
because people don't know how much

00:11:16.092 --> 00:11:20.554
I had put into it. You know, the
conversations I had with players, with

00:11:20.554 --> 00:11:25.101
coaches, the long nights and long days
playing through all type of shit.

00:11:25.101 --> 00:11:25.768
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:11:25.768 --> 00:11:32.358
- So I don't know how much better on and
off the court you could find

00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:33.984
an individual that's going to give it

00:11:33.984 --> 00:11:35.194
they all, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:11:35.194 --> 00:11:36.987
- Like, I didn't care about the politics.

00:11:36.987 --> 00:11:38.781
I didn't care about—all it was about was

00:11:40.074 --> 00:11:45.079
the fans and basketball, but it,
it, it taught me a valuable lesson

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:46.539
of the business that we in.
- Mmhmm.

00:11:46.664 --> 00:11:47.373
- You know what I mean?

00:11:47.373 --> 00:11:50.334
That we all expendable at some
point, you know what I mean?
- Facts.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.212
- We only have a short
window to do what we do.

00:11:53.546 --> 00:11:56.382
You know, it's only a handful of
guys that could control they whole

00:11:56.632 --> 00:11:58.134
destiny through they whole career.

00:11:58.175 --> 00:11:58.884
You know what I mean?

00:11:59.301 --> 00:12:07.268
And I read before, that right before that
or something you met with Masai or you

00:12:07.768 --> 00:12:09.478
- Yeah.
- talked with Masai.

00:12:09.478 --> 00:12:14.024
He told you you weren't getting traded. How long
between y'all conversation and you getting

00:12:14.024 --> 00:12:16.402
traded?
- Two days.
- Two days?
- Two days.

00:12:17.278 --> 00:12:20.239
Um, it was two days. I went to Vegas.

00:12:21.115 --> 00:12:21.949
We all met.

00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:22.700
We had,

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:27.079
had a fucking dinner, you know, have a sit-down
and have a conversation about like,

00:12:27.121 --> 00:12:29.707
all right, we about to do this thing.

00:12:30.332 --> 00:12:32.501
We're about to give it one
more shot, blah, blah, blah.

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:34.712
And two days later, you get a call.

00:12:35.212 --> 00:12:36.464
That was the shit that hurt.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:42.386
Like, tell me beforehand, like, look, it's a
possibility we think about doing this.

00:12:43.012 --> 00:12:43.554
Cool.

00:12:43.721 --> 00:12:44.263
You know what I mean?

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.348
- And your call come from who?
- Masai.

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:47.641
- He tell you you traded?

00:12:47.725 --> 00:12:47.850
- Yeah.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.312
He calls me, so, one, I'm in a
movie theaters. To this day,

00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:52.563
I do not watch this movie,

00:12:53.063 --> 00:12:53.439
right?

00:12:53.481 --> 00:12:56.567
- What's the movie?
- “Equalizer 2.” My phone

00:12:56.567 --> 00:12:57.109
keep ringing.

00:12:58.194 --> 00:12:58.944
It's Masai.

00:12:58.986 --> 00:12:59.779
I'm like, what the fuck?

00:13:00.529 --> 00:13:03.866
So he texted me and said, “Deebo, give
me a call when you get a chance.”

00:13:05.242 --> 00:13:05.618
So I’m like, “All right.

00:13:05.618 --> 00:13:06.076
Cool.”

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.829
So soon as I send a text
back, I get on the Gram.

00:13:09.747 --> 00:13:11.624
I'm like, I don't see
nothin’ on the Gram.

00:13:11.999 --> 00:13:12.833
I'm on Twitter.

00:13:13.209 --> 00:13:14.168
I'm Googling shit.

00:13:14.168 --> 00:13:14.919
So I'm like, all right.

00:13:15.002 --> 00:13:17.046
I don't see, like, you
know, he's just in my mind.

00:13:17.046 --> 00:13:19.089
So I leave the movie theater.

00:13:19.089 --> 00:13:21.258
I get in the car. Call him back.

00:13:21.258 --> 00:13:21.967
He didn't answer.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:26.555
I'm like, probably
ain't nothin’. He call me back.

00:13:28.724 --> 00:13:29.141
And,

00:13:30.893 --> 00:13:33.521
he just, you know, ask how was the movie?

00:13:33.562 --> 00:13:34.605
Like, some casual shit?

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:35.231
You know what I mean?

00:13:35.231 --> 00:13:36.857
Like, so I’m like, “The movie was cool.

00:13:36.857 --> 00:13:37.983
Yeah, what's up?”

00:13:38.025 --> 00:13:39.068
He was like, “Yeah.

00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:42.696
We just traded you to San Antonio.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:45.032
I just want to let you know it’s
going to come out in the morning.”

00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:50.454
And I was like, literally,
bro, I touched the driver.

00:13:50.955 --> 00:13:52.122
I said, “Stop the car.”

00:13:53.791 --> 00:13:58.420
I got out the car and just walked down the
street, hung up the phone, sat outside

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:02.132
at a Jack in the Box or Del
Taco for like two hours.

00:14:02.383 --> 00:14:04.510
Swear to God. Swear to God.

00:14:04.844 --> 00:14:05.803
I called two people.

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:06.887
I called Kyle.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:09.807
He was, he was shocked about it.

00:14:09.807 --> 00:14:10.391
He didn't know.

00:14:10.975 --> 00:14:15.855
Then I called, I called Rudy and
told him like, “Yo, I just got traded.”

00:14:15.855 --> 00:14:17.314
He said, “To where?” I said, “San Antonio.”

00:14:18.274 --> 00:14:19.275
He started laughing,

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:19.650
right?

00:14:20.484 --> 00:14:21.151
I'm like, “Fuck you.”

00:14:21.151 --> 00:14:21.944
I hung up the phone.

00:14:21.944 --> 00:14:24.029
Like, you want to laugh?

00:14:24.029 --> 00:14:24.613
Well, fuck you.

00:14:24.655 --> 00:14:25.614
But you know what I mean?

00:14:25.614 --> 00:14:30.327
But it was, it was, it was one
of the toughest times ever

00:14:30.327 --> 00:14:34.039
because I didn't really have,
like, no guidance with this shit.

00:14:34.164 --> 00:14:34.790
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:37.001
- Like, nobody helped me.

00:14:37.918 --> 00:14:40.045
And I, I wasn't going to
talk to nobody about it.

00:14:40.045 --> 00:14:40.754
You know what I mean?

00:14:40.754 --> 00:14:44.008
I would just, I just wanted to take
it all in and figure it out as it,

00:14:44.091 --> 00:14:45.092
as it went, you know what I mean?

00:14:45.092 --> 00:14:46.343
But it was tough.

00:14:46.427 --> 00:14:51.515
It was tough.
- With you leaving the Raptors,
Kawhi going to the Raptors,

00:14:52.766 --> 00:15:00.232
it feels very similar to us and Steve
Kerr and Mark Jackson. You know, whereas

00:15:00.232 --> 00:15:08.157
like there's always this question of,
well, if Mark was still there—which, the

00:15:08.616 --> 00:15:10.492
year he got fired, we won a championship—

00:15:10.492 --> 00:15:12.077
would they have won a championship?

00:15:13.329 --> 00:15:17.958
And there is no right or wrong
answer. If you were still there

00:15:19.627 --> 00:15:24.131
and Kawhi is still in San Antonio, does
the Raptors end up in that same position?

00:15:24.506 --> 00:15:28.344
Bittersweet moment, watching Kyle
win a championship, uh, watching

00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:31.639
the city that you love winning a
championship, but yet at the same

00:15:31.639 --> 00:15:33.140
time, flipping on your phone and seeing

00:15:34.475 --> 00:15:38.020
people say, “Well, DeMar left and
Kawhi came in and that's why they won.”

00:15:38.354 --> 00:15:39.104
Take me through that.

00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:44.568
- It was definitely a part of me,
you know, feeling the way I'd

00:15:44.568 --> 00:15:45.694
be, you know, it'd be lying.

00:15:45.736 --> 00:15:50.407
It was a part of me saying like, yo,
those guys out there are my partners.

00:15:51.617 --> 00:15:54.703
Like, these my brothers. Like, Kyle, Freddie, Pascal,

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:59.959
OG, like Norm, all those
guys on that team was my dawgs.

00:15:59.959 --> 00:16:00.876
Like, you know what I mean?

00:16:00.876 --> 00:16:05.089
We was, we was like that, and I stayed
in constant communication with them.

00:16:05.172 --> 00:16:05.798
You know what I mean?

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:09.259
And everyone would tell you, like, after
every game, if it was a good game or a

00:16:09.259 --> 00:16:10.844
bad game, I was the first one to hit 'em.

00:16:11.095 --> 00:16:11.845
You know what I mean?

00:16:12.388 --> 00:16:15.849
But, at the same time, you know,
you don't want to look at shit

00:16:16.767 --> 00:16:22.106
social media-wise because everything
was in a comparison of, like, oh,

00:16:23.148 --> 00:16:26.652
DeMar went and did this, or
this week, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:16:26.777 --> 00:16:29.530
- Everything that was happening,
it was frustrating because I

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:30.823
kept getting pulled into it.
- Mmhmm.

00:16:31.115 --> 00:16:31.615
- You know what I mean?

00:16:31.615 --> 00:16:35.077
So once they get to the Finals,
just like, "Oh, DeMar never

00:16:35.077 --> 00:16:36.370
could get 'em to the Finals."

00:16:37.121 --> 00:16:40.582
This, this, and that whole narrative
was just frustrating to where

00:16:40.582 --> 00:16:42.918
it was like, all right, them social media.

00:16:43.293 --> 00:16:46.839
So by the time they got to the
Finals, I think I ended up, like,

00:16:47.423 --> 00:16:50.217
leaving out of the country and just
getting away from everything

00:16:50.259 --> 00:16:55.556
and everybody in my phone. I'm happy to
this day, like, I'm happy for the city.

00:16:55.597 --> 00:17:00.227
I'm happy for, you know, for those guys
that won it and everything, but you

00:17:00.227 --> 00:17:05.441
know, of course that was my—my goal was
to be the one to make that happen.

00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:06.025
You know what I mean?

00:17:07.317 --> 00:17:09.653
But, you know, like, it is what it is.

00:17:10.237 --> 00:17:13.907
- Do you feel because of the way
that played out, because they

00:17:13.907 --> 00:17:15.367
went on to win a championship,

00:17:16.368 --> 00:17:22.041
do you feel more pressure to win a
championship now for you?
- I wouldn't

00:17:22.082 --> 00:17:24.251
necessarily say more pressure.

00:17:24.251 --> 00:17:27.337
Like, I feel the same because, you know,
at the end of the day, like my

00:17:27.337 --> 00:17:32.634
ultimate goal is to win one. But, you know,
it's crazy because whether they won it,

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:36.180
every time somebody won it, you
know, shit, when y'all won it.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.306
Like, my relationship with you
- Mmm.

00:17:37.306 --> 00:17:38.891
- was like, damn, my dog got a ring.

00:17:38.932 --> 00:17:39.850
He got two rings.

00:17:39.892 --> 00:17:40.559
He got three.

00:17:40.601 --> 00:17:41.435
Like, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:17:41.435 --> 00:17:46.523
- Like, same with Klay. Like, the
people that I could go back and

00:17:46.523 --> 00:17:48.108
look at, like, where we came from.

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:49.109
It's, like, you got rings.

00:17:49.109 --> 00:17:52.529
So it's like, you know, you wanna
be part of that group of guys.
- Mmhmm.

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:52.946
- You know what I mean?

00:17:52.946 --> 00:17:54.740
Like, that's the end goal,
you know what I mean?

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:59.453
So whether they won it or not,
like, my...every year it feels

00:18:01.246 --> 00:18:04.041
like you need it more. Like, shit,
we don't play this shit forever.

00:18:04.041 --> 00:18:04.625
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:18:04.875 --> 00:18:07.503
- It's like, once you get it out of the
way it's, like, all right, cool,

00:18:07.503 --> 00:18:08.378
you can breathe now,

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:08.921
you know what I mean?
- Yeah. Facts.

00:18:10.506 --> 00:18:11.006
- For sure, you know what I mean?

00:18:11.006 --> 00:18:15.344
But when you, like, damn, it's my 13th
year. I ain't got one. What's goin' on?

00:18:15.344 --> 00:18:15.928
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:18:15.928 --> 00:18:20.933
So in dealing with all of this, you've
been very outspoken about mental health.

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:22.226
Where is it now?

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:27.147
Have you, have you seen changes in
the way people are operating with it?

00:18:27.147 --> 00:18:28.857
Is it kinda the same as it's still,

00:18:30.234 --> 00:18:32.736
you know, an unthought of
thing in the league?

00:18:32.778 --> 00:18:37.699
Where is that now in, in the progression,
since you first started speaking on it?

00:18:38.325 --> 00:18:41.995
- The conversation and the topic and the
narrative is definitely a priority.

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:48.585
So with that being a priority, it definitely
made so much progress because before then,

00:18:48.752 --> 00:18:52.714
you know, especially in our sport, it was
never talked about. You know, nobody never

00:18:52.714 --> 00:18:54.967
talked about the situation that we all

00:18:56.051 --> 00:19:00.973
have. For one, we come into the league
with so much trauma that we don't

00:19:00.973 --> 00:19:03.517
even identify with from our childhood.

00:19:03.725 --> 00:19:04.268
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:19:04.268 --> 00:19:08.272
- But we suppress it and forget about it so
easy because all of a sudden now we rich.
- Mmhmm.

00:19:08.897 --> 00:19:12.943
- But now with us all of a sudden getting
money, now we have a new set of problems.
- Yeah.

00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:16.363
- We haven't even uncovered the whole shit

00:19:16.363 --> 00:19:18.574
we went through 20 years prior.

00:19:18.615 --> 00:19:19.199
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:19:19.199 --> 00:19:21.118
- Now we forced to be in a spotlight.

00:19:21.827 --> 00:19:25.706
Have fame, have money, have attention
to have even more temptations and

00:19:25.706 --> 00:19:30.627
more problems that come with that,
that we gathering more fucking

00:19:30.794 --> 00:19:38.427
bullshit that, like, will build up
unconsciously, and you will see outbursts or

00:19:38.427 --> 00:19:42.097
certain, you know, reactions from people,

00:19:42.097 --> 00:19:45.601
and you wonder why? At our
age, bro, like, we was taught to

00:19:46.602 --> 00:19:47.394
be tough.

00:19:47.561 --> 00:19:49.229
- Absolutely. Fucking power through

00:19:49.229 --> 00:19:49.813
everything.

00:19:49.855 --> 00:19:50.105
- Yeah.

00:19:50.147 --> 00:19:51.607
Like, man, fuck all that shit

00:19:51.607 --> 00:19:53.859
you going through you. You
gotta get through this shit.
- Mmhmm.

00:19:54.026 --> 00:19:56.320
- Ain't no crying. You better
suck it up and keep going.

00:19:56.445 --> 00:19:57.070
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:04.161
- Like, but, you know, we both fathers now,
and you see the difference on how to be

00:20:04.161 --> 00:20:08.540
with your own children because, you know,
shit, that's not necessarily a right way.

00:20:08.582 --> 00:20:09.249
You know what I mean?
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:20:09.625 --> 00:20:10.751
- That's not the right way.

00:20:10.751 --> 00:20:11.543
And my biggest thing is

00:20:12.628 --> 00:20:15.923
just having players in our
position share stories that they

00:20:15.923 --> 00:20:20.510
went through because that shit is
inspiring to the next generation.

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:21.011
You know what I mean?

00:20:21.011 --> 00:20:26.183
It's a different type of, you
know, inspiration that you give and

00:20:26.183 --> 00:20:30.729
confidence that you give that,
you know, these kids knowing that

00:20:30.771 --> 00:20:32.105
it's OK, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:35.442
- It’s OK. You going to go
through this, but now identify

00:20:35.692 --> 00:20:36.777
with it and understand it,

00:20:36.777 --> 00:20:38.779
so you don't fall victim to, you know,

00:20:40.405 --> 00:20:43.992
the aggression and wondering why you
had this aggression or this hatred

00:20:43.992 --> 00:20:48.163
or this or your, your confidence, not
there, or your insecurities and all

00:20:48.163 --> 00:20:49.289
this stuff that could come with it.

00:20:49.331 --> 00:20:53.043
Mental health does not
discriminate on nobody in life.
- Mmhmm.

00:20:53.168 --> 00:20:53.794
- You know what I mean?

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:59.508
I realized that as Black men,
we always felt like we was in

00:20:59.675 --> 00:21:01.093
competition with one another.

00:21:01.093 --> 00:21:04.263
Like, I'm not about to let you
know what I'm going through.
- Yeah.
- For what?

00:21:04.388 --> 00:21:06.473
Like, you probably use that against me.

00:21:06.515 --> 00:21:07.432
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:21:07.557 --> 00:21:11.561
- Instead of understanding like,
what's going on, bro? Like, it just

00:21:12.145 --> 00:21:15.649
bounce off conversations and realize
how much shit you could get off

00:21:15.691 --> 00:21:18.235
each other's chest that tomorrow

00:21:18.235 --> 00:21:20.779
you're going to take a different approach
with something that you normally do

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.741
that bothers you and approach it the
right way to get through it easier.

00:21:24.741 --> 00:21:25.367
You know what I mean?

00:21:25.575 --> 00:21:26.285
- Absolutely.

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:31.581
Nike recently gave every Nike
employee, I think, a week off.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:36.628
Um, and it was centered
around their mental health.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:41.383
Like, everybody take a week and
kind of gather yourself recharged.

00:21:41.508 --> 00:21:44.219
We saw an instance like that last season

00:21:45.929 --> 00:21:49.141
where Kyrie essentially went on a hiatus.

00:21:49.224 --> 00:21:52.477
I think it was maybe seven to 10
days of where he's like, no, I'm

00:21:52.519 --> 00:21:54.021
just not mentally in a good place.
- Yeah.

00:21:54.021 --> 00:21:55.939
- And I need to step away from basketball.

00:21:57.441 --> 00:22:02.904
Is that something that the NBA need, like
similar to what Nike just did? Because,

00:22:02.946 --> 00:22:04.072
you know, we get an All-Star break,

00:22:04.072 --> 00:22:04.364
right?

00:22:04.364 --> 00:22:07.701
But number one, if you're an
All-Star, it's not a break.
- Not a break, yeah.

00:22:07.701 --> 00:22:10.537
- Do you see that as something that's coming
to the league that need to be in

00:22:10.537 --> 00:22:12.080
the league as someone who has

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:14.207
struggled with mental health?

00:22:14.249 --> 00:22:15.834
Do we need a mental health week?

00:22:16.335 --> 00:22:16.752
- Yeah.

00:22:16.752 --> 00:22:20.964
And I think that comes with a
conversation of asking what guys need.

00:22:21.006 --> 00:22:22.257
Everybody needs something different.

00:22:22.257 --> 00:22:22.716
You know what I mean?

00:22:22.716 --> 00:22:25.510
Like for me, my hoopin’
is my therapeutic relief.

00:22:25.594 --> 00:22:26.011
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:22:26.011 --> 00:22:29.264
- Sometimes I, I just want to be out
there hoop, you know what I mean?

00:22:29.264 --> 00:22:33.894
Like, like in, in be around that type
of environment, you know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:22:33.935 --> 00:22:36.772
- That's, that's, that's for me.
Some people may be different to

00:22:36.772 --> 00:22:39.941
where, like, I just need to step
away and not see a basketball for,

00:22:40.734 --> 00:22:42.319
you know, a certain amount of
days, you know what I mean?

00:22:42.319 --> 00:22:45.781
I think that's a conversation
that should be had around people

00:22:45.781 --> 00:22:49.284
who understand what they need

00:22:49.409 --> 00:22:52.329
so they don't fall in
that, in that black hole.

00:22:52.996 --> 00:22:59.002
- What is the happiest you've been
playing basketball?

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.046
- Basketball, it's always made me happy.

00:23:01.046 --> 00:23:03.507
Just the opportunity
to play, honestly, bro.

00:23:04.049 --> 00:23:05.550
I never take it for granted, bro.

00:23:06.843 --> 00:23:08.136
And it’s like, just knowing where I came from.

00:23:08.136 --> 00:23:11.598
Just knowing that I'm able
to be in the league this long.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.934
Like, that's my happy place.

00:23:14.267 --> 00:23:17.354
It's one day we going to have to
hang this motherfucker up.
- Facts.

00:23:17.437 --> 00:23:22.109
- So every time I get a chance to play,
it's like, yo, this, you gotta enjoy it.

00:23:22.192 --> 00:23:22.943
You gotta enjoy it.

00:23:23.318 --> 00:23:26.405
- How much longer you wanna go?

00:23:26.405 --> 00:23:28.698
- Fasho not no 20 years. I
ain't trying to play no 20.

00:23:28.698 --> 00:23:32.494
I can't do 20, you know?
I ain't trying to—hey,

00:23:34.871 --> 00:23:35.831
I don't know, bro.

00:23:35.831 --> 00:23:39.292
I don't even want to put a number
on it, but 20 is a long time.
- It’s a long time.

00:23:39.501 --> 00:23:41.378
- I definitely don't want
to be playing at 40.

00:23:41.920 --> 00:23:44.047
I don't want to be 40
years old, still playing.

00:23:44.047 --> 00:23:49.010
I commend Vince and all the
guys who played till they was 40.

00:23:49.261 --> 00:23:51.805
I just don't think mentally and physically

00:23:51.805 --> 00:23:52.514
I want to do it.

00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:55.600
- Hey, I appreciate you coming out, man.

00:23:55.600 --> 00:24:00.564
I appreciate you coming on the show. As
my brother, it’s always special, uh, to

00:24:00.564 --> 00:24:01.982
sit down and have these conversations.

00:24:02.774 --> 00:24:04.025
I don't take this for granted.

00:24:04.109 --> 00:24:05.610
I take it extremely serious.

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:06.820
I'm very appreciative.

00:24:07.279 --> 00:24:08.280
Thank you for coming on.

00:24:08.572 --> 00:24:10.157
- Man, always.
- “Chips,”

00:24:10.198 --> 00:24:10.991
that's a wrap.

